(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-cms-exam-5-master-exam-form-e"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 5 Master Exam — Form E","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 76-year-old man has had symmetric pitting swelling of both ankles for three weeks. He started amlodipine for hypertension one month ago. There is no warmth, redness, tenderness or skin discoloration, and he has no history of heart, kidney or liver disease. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Bilateral deep vein thrombosis","Drug-induced edema","Chronic venous insufficiency","Lymphedema"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Swelling that is often bilateral, without signs of inflammation, is a common adverse effect of some drugs, including dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers such as amlodipine. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man with Mobitz type II block found on a routine tracing declines a pacemaker because he feels perfectly well. Which risk should be explained to him?","choices":["Gradual return to normal","Progression to complete block","Easing into first-degree block","Atrial fibrillation and stroke"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mobitz type II carries a higher risk of progression to complete heart block and sudden cardiac death, and it is considered dangerous even without symptoms. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman with recurrent urinary tract infections was treated for pyelonephritis a month ago. She now has low-grade fevers, fatigue and a new murmur, and blood cultures grow gram-positive cocci. Which organism is most likely?","choices":["Enterococci","Staphylococcus epidermidis","Viridans streptococci","Streptococcus gallolyticus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The genitourinary tract is the source of enterococci, which cause subacute and native valve endocarditis; urinary tract infection gives them a route into the blood. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman has had a resting heart rate of 105 to 115 for a year. Thyroid tests, a blood count, a drug screen and an echocardiogram are normal, and she takes no medications or stimulants. Her electrocardiogram shows a normal P wave before every narrow QRS. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Atrial flutter with 2:1 block","Atrioventricular nodal reentry","Focal atrial tachycardia","Inappropriate sinus tachycardia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A sinus tachycardia that remains unexplained after secondary causes are excluded is inappropriate sinus tachycardia, a diagnosis of exclusion. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman without diabetes has ulcers on the posterior aspect of both lower legs. Her pedal pulses are strong, and there are no varicose veins, brown pigmentation or leg swelling. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Arterial ulcer","Diabetic neuropathic ulcer","Venous ulcer","Erythema induratum"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Bilateral ulcers on the posterior aspect of the lower leg, with intact pulses and no venous skin changes, is the pattern listed for erythema induratum in the differential of leg ulcers. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man has a 4.8 cm descending thoracic aortic aneurysm, and he now has back pain that is attributed to the aneurysm. Which management is most appropriate?","choices":["Repair only above 6.0 cm","Repair the aneurysm","Imaging every 3 to 5 years","Imaging every 6 to 12 months"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Repair is recommended for all symptomatic thoracic aortic aneurysms, whatever the diameter; size thresholds apply only to asymptomatic ones. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman with restrictive cardiomyopathy and marked biatrial enlargement has had palpitations for a week, and the electrocardiogram now shows atrial fibrillation. Which treatment reduces her risk of embolism?","choices":["Surgical myectomy","A positive inotrope","Aggressive diuresis","Anticoagulation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Atrial fibrillation is common in restrictive cardiomyopathy because of atrial enlargement, it raises the risk of atrial thrombus, and anticoagulants may be needed to prevent embolism from atrial thrombi. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman has sudden palpitations after three energy drinks. She notices her neck throbbing, and examination shows a frog sign (rapid visible pulsations in the neck). Her heart rate is 172 and regular, with a narrow QRS and no visible P waves. She has no heart disease. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Atrioventricular nodal reentry","Atrial fibrillation","Sinus tachycardia","Multifocal atrial tachycardia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A paroxysmal, regular narrow tachycardia with hidden P waves, neck pulsations (the frog sign) and a stimulant trigger in a young woman without heart disease is atrioventricular nodal reentrant tachycardia. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman being treated for an acute aortic dissection develops a new diastolic murmur and worsening breathlessness. Which complication explains these findings?","choices":["Aortic regurgitation","Carotid stenosis","Mitral stenosis","Pseudoaneurysm"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Aortic dissection can cause aortic regurgitation through dilatation of the aortic root, producing a diastolic murmur and breathlessness. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 116"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with previous infective endocarditis brings an old wallet card that lists clindamycin for her dental prophylaxis. She has a penicillin allergy. Which counseling point is most accurate?","choices":["She no longer needs any","Keep using clindamycin","Clindamycin is dropped","Switch to amoxicillin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Clindamycin is no longer recommended for prophylaxis because of the risk of Clostridioides difficile infection; she should be given an alternative such as azithromycin. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man has had a peripheral intravenous catheter in his right forearm for six days. He now has a fever of 39.2 degrees Celsius, a tender cord along the vein, purulent drainage at the insertion site, and redness that extends several centimeters beyond the vein margin. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Uncomplicated superficial thrombophlebitis","Septic thrombophlebitis","Cellulitis alone","Upper extremity deep vein thrombosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. High fever, purulent drainage and erythema extending well beyond the margin of the vein after cannulation indicate infection within the vein, called suppurative or septic thrombophlebitis. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man with known obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy has a crescendo-decrescendo systolic murmur at the left sternal border that radiates to the suprasternal notch. At the bedside, the examiner tests how the murmur responds to changes in blood flow through the heart. Which maneuver would make his murmur louder?","choices":["Valsalva maneuver","Passive leg raise","Sustained handgrip","Rapid squatting"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Anything that decreases blood flow in the heart increases the outflow obstruction and the murmur. The Valsalva maneuver and standing reduce preload, so the murmur of obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy gets louder. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"Acute pericarditis accounts for about what share of emergency department admissions for nonischemic chest pain?","choices":["About 1%","About 5%","About 25%","About 50%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acute pericarditis is recorded in about 5% of patients admitted through the emergency department for nonischemic chest pain, with an incidence near 27.7 per 100,000 a year. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which lifestyle measures are advised for adults with cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Caffeine avoidance and deliberate weight gain","Sodium restriction and alcohol cessation","Strict bed rest and total fluid restriction","Fluid loading and a high-salt diet plan"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Adult lifestyle modifications are sodium restriction and alcohol cessation, alongside guideline-directed medical therapy and device therapy. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"Which anticoagulation strategy is described as the classical, waning approach?","choices":["Heparin bridged to warfarin","Apixaban with a loading period","Parenteral therapy, then dabigatran","Oral rivaroxaban with loading"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The classical strategy starts with heparin and bridges to warfarin; it is cheap and reliable but needs frequent laboratory follow-up of the international normalized ratio. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A 15-year-old girl nearly fainted twice while standing in the lunch line; both times she felt better once she lay down. She plays competitive soccer. A systolic murmur is heard at the left sternal border that becomes louder with the Valsalva maneuver. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Clear her to play next week","Recheck the murmur in a year","Urgent echocardiography","Reassure; common in teenagers"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Presyncope on standing relieved by lying down, like syncope, identifies patients with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy at high risk of sudden death, so the work-up is urgent, and echocardiography is the typical test of choice. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man received chest radiation for lymphoma 20 years ago. He has 8 months of exertional dyspnea, fatigue, leg edema and ascites. His jugular venous pressure is elevated and shows the Kussmaul sign (it rises on inspiration instead of falling), and a pericardial knock (an early diastolic sound, slightly earlier than a third heart sound) is heard. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Constrictive pericarditis","Infective endocarditis","Acute pericarditis","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Fluid overload with low output, an elevated jugular venous pressure with the Kussmaul sign and a pericardial knock after prior radiation are the picture of constrictive pericarditis, scarring that limits filling. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with claudication, diabetes and continued smoking is taking a statin. Which low-density lipoprotein target applies to him?","choices":["Below 70 mg/dL","Below 100 mg/dL","Below 130 mg/dL","Below 160 mg/dL"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Lipid-lowering therapy in peripheral artery disease targets a low-density lipoprotein below 100 mg/dL, and below 70 mg/dL for high-risk patients such as those with diabetes or continued smoking. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Uremic pericarditis is typically associated with a blood urea nitrogen above which level?","choices":["10 mg/dL","20 mg/dL","60 mg/dL","200 mg/dL"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Uremic pericarditis is linked to a blood urea nitrogen above 60 mg/dL; there will be laboratory evidence and a history of kidney disease, and it is treated with dialysis. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 22-year-old man has recovered from mild viral myocarditis and feels well at discharge. He asks whether he needs any long-term follow-up with a cardiologist. Which later complication does follow-up watch for?","choices":["Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Takotsubo cardiomyopathy","Dilated cardiomyopathy","Restrictive cardiomyopathy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Most patients with mild myocarditis recover completely, but one third subsequently develop dilated cardiomyopathy, and viral myocarditis accounts for about 20% of dilated cardiomyopathy cases. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"An irregular, wide-complex tachycardia with some rates approaching 300 appears in someone with a known accessory pathway. Which intravenous drug is used?","choices":["Procainamide","Magnesium","Atropine","Adenosine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An irregular, wide-complex tachycardia approaching 300 with an accessory pathway is atrial fibrillation with Wolff-Parkinson-White (ventricular tachycardia would be regular at 150 to 200), treated with intravenous procainamide, never adenosine. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman has been in the hospital for 2 weeks with a peripherally inserted central line. She develops fever, and two separate blood cultures grow a gram-positive coccus with no other source. Which organism causes the largest share of this setting's native valve endocarditis?","choices":["Enterococci","Viridans streptococci","Staphylococcus aureus","Coagulase-negative staphylococci"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Staphylococcus aureus causes about 52% of health care-associated native valve endocarditis, and methicillin resistance is common among these strains. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What is the mainstay of treatment for acute myocarditis?","choices":["Long antibiotics","Early transplant","Septal ablation","Supportive care"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Treatment is directed at the clinical scenario and supportive care is the mainstay, with aggressive hemodynamic stabilization when needed and drugs as required. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman on chemotherapy for breast cancer comes to the emergency department with a calf 4 cm larger than the other, tenderness along the deep veins and no better explanation, so her Wells score is high. Her D-dimer, drawn at triage, is negative. Which action should be avoided?","choices":["Ordering a venous duplex ultrasound","Measuring both calf circumferences","Asking about shortness of breath","Ending the work-up at the D-dimer"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. With a high Wells score the D-dimer is skipped, or if it was drawn, a negative result does not end the evaluation: she still goes to venous duplex ultrasound. Stopping here risks missing a clot with high pretest probability. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 54-year-old woman who started a new antibiotic three days ago faints briefly. Her electrocardiogram before the event showed a markedly prolonged QT. The monitor now shows a wide QRS tachycardia whose peaks rise and fall so that the complexes seem to twist around the baseline. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Coarse ventricular fibrillation","Atrial flutter","Monomorphic ventricular tachycardia","Torsades de pointes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Polymorphic ventricular tachycardia whose QRS complexes appear to twist around the baseline, on a background of a long QT, is torsades de pointes; most cases come from acquired long QT, often drug-induced. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man is brought in confused, with a blood pressure of 76/40. The monitor shows a slow, regular rhythm at 36 with a normal P wave before every narrow QRS. Which is the first-line treatment?","choices":["Permanent pacemaker","Synchronized cardioversion","Atropine","Adenosine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Acute, symptomatic, hemodynamically unstable sinus bradycardia is treated first with atropine; if it fails, a temporary pacemaker follows, and chronic symptomatic disease gets a permanent one. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 30-year-old cyclist with no symptoms has a rhythm in which the PR interval lengthens beat by beat until a QRS complex is dropped, after which the cycle starts again. Echocardiogram is normal. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["No treatment needed","Catheter ablation","Transcutaneous pacing","Permanent pacemaker"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Asymptomatic Mobitz type I block needs no treatment; a permanent pacemaker is considered only for debilitating symptoms or heart failure symptoms. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which electrolyte disturbances are named triggers of premature ventricular contractions?","choices":["Low potassium, low magnesium","High potassium, low sodium","Low calcium, high sodium","High phosphate, low chloride"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hypokalemia and hypomagnesemia, along with hypercalcemia, are the electrolyte imbalances named as causes of premature ventricular contractions. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 4-year-old with cyanotic congenital heart disease and a systemic-to-pulmonary shunt has 2 weeks of fever. The murmur sounds unchanged. Which finding would most suggest that the shunt is infected?","choices":["An unchanged murmur","Falling oxygen saturation","Rising blood pressure","Osler nodes on fingers"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In children with cyanotic congenital heart disease and systemic-pulmonary shunts the murmur may not change; falling oxygen saturation can signal graft infection. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 73-year-old man has new pauses and slow rates on an ambulatory monitor. He started verapamil and digoxin last month for palpitations. He feels mildly lightheaded but is otherwise well. Which is the most appropriate first step?","choices":["Stop the rate-slowing drugs","Atropine tablets","Permanent pacemaker now","Catheter ablation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Medications (beta blockers, nondihydropyridine calcium channel blockers and digoxin) are listed causes of sick sinus syndrome, and management starts by treating the underlying cause. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with diabetes who smokes wakes most nights with burning pain in his forefoot and toes that eases when he hangs the leg over the bed; he now sleeps in a recliner. Which ankle-brachial index range would be expected?","choices":["0.4 to 0.9","0 to 0.4","Above 0.9 to 1.3","Above 1.3"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Rest pain and tissue loss (ulcer, gangrene) correspond to an ankle-brachial index of 0 to 0.4, the critical limb ischemia band, well below the claudication range. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man has an asymptomatic abdominal aortic aneurysm measuring 4.7 cm on his latest ultrasound. Which referral is appropriate?","choices":["Emergency surgery this evening","None until it reaches 5.5 cm","Vascular referral, for observation","Cardiology, for echocardiography"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Aneurysms of 4.5 cm or larger warrant referral to a vascular team for observation and assessment, with urgent referral whenever the patient has aneurysmal pain, whatever the size. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman was admitted with chest pain after a near drowning. Her electrocardiogram showed ST elevation with T-wave inversion, and echocardiography showed hypokinesis of the midsegment and apex of the left ventricle. Which catheterization finding is expected if this is stress cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Normal coronary arteries","Triple-vessel narrowing","A proximal coronary occlusion","A ruptured coronary plaque"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Stress cardiomyopathy mimics acute coronary syndrome but lacks angiographic coronary artery disease or acute plaque rupture; catheterization most often shows normal coronary arteries. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man in cardiac arrest is intubated by the team. Compressions continue. How should breaths now be given?","choices":["One breath every 2 seconds","Compressions without breaths","Two breaths per 30 compressions","One breath every 6 seconds"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Once intubated, cardiopulmonary resuscitation continues as continuous chest compressions with 1 breath every 6 seconds. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What is the first-line treatment for stable supraventricular tachycardia?","choices":["Intravenous procainamide","Synchronized cardioversion","A vagal maneuver","Adenosine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Stable supraventricular tachycardia is treated first with a vagal maneuver, then adenosine (which may be repeated once); an unstable patient gets immediate synchronized cardioversion. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"How is polymorphic ventricular tachycardia treated, even when the patient has a pulse?","choices":["Synchronized cardioversion","Defibrillation","Observation on a monitor","Intravenous lidocaine alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Polymorphic ventricular tachycardia is never truly stable, only a short-lived transitional state, so it is defibrillated even if the patient looks well. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"In high-income countries, which conditions are now the leading predisposing conditions for infective endocarditis?","choices":["Hypertension and high cholesterol","Rheumatic heart disease and syphilis","Atrial fibrillation and heart block","Degenerative valve disease and devices"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Degenerative valve disease and intracardiac devices are now the leading predisposing conditions in high-income countries; rheumatic heart disease stays on the risk factor list. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 28-year-old man had a cold 10 days ago. He now has sharp, pleuritic (worse with a deep breath) substernal chest pain that is worse lying flat and eases when he leans forward. A pericardial friction rub (a scratchy, squeaking sound) is heard at the left sternal border, and the electrocardiogram shows widespread concave ST elevation with PR depression and ST depression in aVR. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acute pericarditis","Anterior myocardial infarction","Viral pleuritis","Acute pulmonary embolism"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Sharp pleuritic, positional pain after a viral illness, a friction rub and widespread concave ST elevation with PR depression (reciprocal changes in aVR) meet well over two of the four diagnostic criteria for acute pericarditis. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What is the common site of a pseudoaneurysm?","choices":["Popliteal artery after bypass","Femoral artery after catheterization","Carotid bulb after endarterectomy","Infrarenal aorta in smokers"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The common site is the femoral artery after cardiac catheterization, caused by laceration of the artery by the access needle. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman with a stab wound to the abdomen arrives pulseless after losing a large amount of blood. The monitor shows an organized narrow rhythm. Compressions are underway. Besides compressions and epinephrine, which problem must be addressed urgently?","choices":["Hypothermia","Toxin ingestion","Hyperkalemia","Hypovolemia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The two leading causes of pulseless electrical activity in adults are hypovolemia and hypoxia; her massive blood loss is hypovolemia that must be corrected. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman has abrupt-onset palpitations during a week of little sleep and heavy coffee. Her heart rate is 160 and regular, and every QRS is preceded by an identical P wave whose shape differs from her baseline P wave. She has no heart disease. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Atrial flutter","Sinus tachycardia","Focal atrial tachycardia","Multifocal atrial tachycardia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A regular tachycardia with one consistent, abnormally shaped P wave before each QRS, starting abruptly in someone without heart disease under increased metabolic demand, is focal atrial tachycardia. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman had a bioprosthetic aortic valve placed 5 months ago. She now has a fever of 39.0 °C (102.2 °F) and a new regurgitant murmur, and blood cultures have been drawn. Which empiric regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Penicillin G plus gentamicin","Vancomycin, gentamicin and cefepime","Vancomycin plus ceftriaxone","Ceftriaxone alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. When a prosthetic valve has been in place for 1 year or less, empiric therapy is vancomycin plus gentamicin plus cefepime. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 53-year-old man on the cardiology ward develops jugular venous distension and a blood pressure of 88/60 a day after a catheter procedure. Before drainage, which bedside triad should be specifically checked for?","choices":["Fever, a new murmur and splinters","Hypotension, neck veins, muffled sounds","Hypertension, bradycardia, apnea","Alternans, Kussmaul sign, a knock"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The Beck triad is decreased arterial pressure, distended jugular veins and distant (muffled) heart sounds, the three bedside signs of cardiac tamponade to check before drainage. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man with a first deep vein thrombosis is otherwise healthy and hemodynamically stable. The plan is oral anticoagulation monotherapy with a loading phase followed by maintenance, without any injection lead-in. Which drug fits this strategy?","choices":["Fondaparinux","Edoxaban","Dabigatran","Apixaban"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Apixaban, a direct factor Xa inhibitor, is given as oral monotherapy with a loading dose followed by a maintenance dose, one of the three major anticoagulation strategies. Rivaroxaban follows the same pattern. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"An 82-year-old man is confused, with a blood pressure of 74/40 and a heart rate of 32. The electrocardiogram shows P waves and wide QRS complexes at two separate regular rates with no relationship between them. Which is the most appropriate immediate measure?","choices":["Synchronized cardioversion","Defibrillation","Transcutaneous pacing","Intravenous amiodarone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Unstable complete heart block is treated emergently with transcutaneous pacing, pads on the chest, as a bridge to the definitive permanent pacemaker. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman with atrial fibrillation who takes no anticoagulant develops sudden, severe pain in her left leg two hours ago. The leg is cold and pale, pulses are absent below the femoral artery, and she has numbness over the dorsum of the foot. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Critical limb ischemia","Acute embolic limb ischemia","Intermittent claudication","Atheroembolism with blue toes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sudden occlusion of a previously patent artery with abrupt severe pain and absent pulses is acute limb ischemia; the heart is the embolic source in 80 to 90% of cases, atrial fibrillation being a classic example. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man is diagnosed with a first proximal deep vein thrombosis five days after knee replacement under two hours of general anesthesia. His clot is attributed to the venous stasis and endothelial trauma of the Virchow triad, both from the surgery. What duration of anticoagulation is appropriate?","choices":["Indefinitely","Six weeks","Three to six months","Three months"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A clot provoked by a major transient, reversible risk factor, such as surgery under general anesthesia for more than 30 minutes, is treated for three months. Three months is also the minimum initial duration for any first venous thromboembolism. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman has had three episodes of pericarditis in 18 months, each treated in primary care with an anti-inflammatory and colchicine. Which referral is most appropriate?","choices":["Cardiology","Cardiothoracic surgery","Gastroenterology","Infectious disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Referral to cardiology is advised for recurrences (recurrent pericarditis), for no response to conservative management and for the development of constrictive pericarditis. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 26-year-old marathon runner is diagnosed with acute viral pericarditis and starts ibuprofen and colchicine. He asks when he can return to training. Which advice is most appropriate?","choices":["Avoid exercise for the rest of his life","Resume running once the chest pain stops","Run on alternate days during treatment","Avoid strenuous activity for at least 1 month"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Strenuous activity should be avoided until symptoms resolve and biomarkers such as C-reactive protein normalize, at least 1 month after diagnosis, keeping the maximal heart rate under 100. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman has needed prednisone for several months for pericarditis that did not respond to first-line therapy. Which complication of her treatment should be specifically anticipated?","choices":["Bradycardia","Hypoglycemia","Osteoporosis","Hearing loss"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Corticosteroids increase the risk of osteoporosis, a particular concern in older adults on prolonged courses, so bone health must be addressed. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 51-year-old man with dilated cardiomyopathy drinks most of a bottle of whiskey every night. He is on a beta blocker and an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor, his ejection fraction is 30%, and he asks whether anything he does himself could help his heart recover. Which counseling point is most accurate?","choices":["Damage is fixed once the heart dilates","Stopping alcohol may let function recover","Wine would be safer than spirits for him","Only a transplant could restore function"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. With long-term excess alcohol as the cause, cardiac function can recover if intake is reduced or stopped, and removing the offending source is part of patient education for dilated cardiomyopathy. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman has a new moderate pericardial effusion with joint aches and fatigue. She has not traveled and has no recent viral illness. Which test is most reasonable to add to her workup?","choices":["Blood cultures","Hemoglobin A1c","Serum lipase","Antinuclear antibody"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The effusion workup includes blood counts, chemistry, thyroid-stimulating hormone and troponins, and possibly an antinuclear antibody, for example in a young woman with an effusion, to look for lupus. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with advanced dilated cardiomyopathy has refractory symptoms on full medical therapy. Severe lung disease and frailty rule him out as a transplant candidate, and he wants to remain at home and mobile. Which treatment option fits his situation?","choices":["Surgical myectomy of the septum","Urgent listing for cardiac transplant","Assist device as destination therapy","Assist device as a bridge to transplant"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Destination therapy with a left ventricular assist device is for patients who are not candidates for cardiac transplantation. Patients may live more than five years with the device and can stay mobile. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which feature distinguishes Osler nodes from Janeway lesions?","choices":["Osler nodes are painless","Osler nodes persist for months","Osler nodes are painful","Osler nodes are retinal"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Osler nodes are painful, an immunologic phenomenon; Janeway lesions are nontender, a vascular (embolic) phenomenon. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man who injects heroin has 4 days of fever to 39.6 °C (103.3 °F), rigors and a cough. A new holosystolic murmur is heard at the left lower sternal border, and he looks toxic. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Mitral valve endocarditis","Community-acquired pneumonia","Tricuspid valve endocarditis","Acute viral pericarditis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In people who inject drugs the tricuspid valve is the valve singled out (Staphylococcus aureus causes 80-90% of those infections); cough is linked to this group, and a holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border fits the tricuspid valve. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man is recovering from a carotid endarterectomy for high-grade symptomatic stenosis. Which complications of the procedure should be monitored for?","choices":["Rupture into the retroperitoneum","Stroke or nerve damage","Graft infection of the leg","Wet gangrene of the foot"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Carotid endarterectomy removes plaque through a surgical incision and is the gold standard for high-grade stenosis; its named complications are stroke and nerve damage. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"A 79-year-old man has a preoperative electrocardiogram before cataract surgery. He feels well. His heart rate is 70, every P wave is followed by a narrow QRS complex, and the PR interval is constant at 0.26 seconds on every beat. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["First-degree block","Mobitz type I block","Mobitz type II block","Complete heart block"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A constant PR interval longer than 0.20 seconds with every P wave conducted to a narrow QRS is first-degree atrioventricular block, most often from age-related degeneration of the conduction system. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman has atrial fibrillation found at a routine visit. Her heart rate is 84, she has no symptoms of instability, and her blood pressure is 128/76. Which is the most appropriate rate management?","choices":["Synchronized cardioversion","Atropine","Oral rate control","Intravenous rate control"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A hemodynamically stable patient in atrial fibrillation without rapid ventricular response is managed with rate control by mouth, with anticoagulation guided by stroke risk. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man with a first calf deep vein thrombosis is hemodynamically stable, has low bleeding risk and normal kidney function, and has a supportive spouse who will bring him to every follow-up visit. Which is the best setting for treatment?","choices":["Observation without treatment","Hospital admission for heparin","Intensive care unit monitoring","Outpatient anticoagulation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Outpatient therapy suits patients who are hemodynamically stable, have low bleeding risk and no renal insufficiency, and have good support with reliable follow-up. He meets every criterion. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman has frequent premature junctional contractions, more than 10 per minute, on a monitor. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Anticoagulation","Permanent pacemaker","Reassurance alone","Check for heart disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Frequent premature junctional contractions (more than 10 per minute, or multifocal) prompt a search for structural heart disease, with a beta blocker to treat them. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 61"}]};
